# railway up uploads this directory from the dev machine before the image is
# built. The image rebuilds all of it itself (see the Dockerfile: npm ci +
# build:demo for the SPA, pip install for the backend), so shipping the venv,
# node_modules, the build output and the git history only made the upload time
# out - the tree is 3.2G and the transfer never finished.
#
# Mirrors .dockerignore, plus the build outputs that the image produces on its
# own. Kept in sync with it deliberately: an upload that carries something the
# image ignores is just wasted bandwidth.

# --- rebuilt inside the image ---------------------------------------------
.venv/
**/.venv/
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
**/dist/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# --- local data that must never leave the machine --------------------------
# attachments/ is 2.7G of uploaded request files. The deployed service keeps its
# own on the mounted volume; shipping this one both timed the upload out and
# would have overwritten the server's data with a dev machine's copy.
attachments/
**/attachments/
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite3.*
*.bak-*
db.sqlite3*

# --- dev caches ------------------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.git/

# --- secrets — never leave the machine -------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/auth.json
**/auth.lock

# --- noise -----------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
